WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:06.500
<v Instructor>Good morning everyone, today we are going to continue with convolutional neural networks.

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:12.400
<v Instructor>A convolution is defined as a sliding dot product between a small filter and a local patch of the input.

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:19.800
<v Instructor>The filter is also called the kernel, and the output of this operation is called a feature map.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:26.300
<v Student>Sir, how do we choose the size of the kernel?

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:33.900
<v Instructor>That is a good question. The kernel size is a hyperparameter, and for most image tasks three by three is the default choice.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:41.200
<v Instructor>Stride controls how far the filter moves at each step, and padding preserves the spatial dimensions at the borders.

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:48.700
<v Instructor>For example, with a five by five input, a three by three kernel, stride one and no padding, the output is three by three.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:55.400
<v Instructor>Max pooling keeps the strongest activation in a region and discards the rest, which reduces the spatial size.

00:00:55.500 --> 00:01:02.800
<v Student>Does pooling always improve accuracy?

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:10.100
<v Instructor>Not always. Some people argue that pooling throws away information, and recent architectures often replace it with strided convolutions.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:17.600
<v Instructor>Let us discuss the trade-off, because it matters for your assignment design.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:24.900
<v Instructor>On the one hand, pooling gives translation invariance and cuts computation. On the other hand, it loses precise location information.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:32.300
<v Instructor>We have decided that this semester we will use strided convolutions instead of pooling in the final project.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:39.800
<v Instructor>Before we move on, please note that the assignment deadline is September 23, and you must submit a working notebook.

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:47.200
<v Instructor>Assignment 3 covers kernel size selection, stride arithmetic and pooling. Submit it before 23rd September at 11:59 pm.

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:54.600
<v Instructor>Remember to include a one page write up explaining your design choices.

00:01:54.700 --> 00:02:01.100
<v Instructor>There will be a short quiz on convolution arithmetic at the start of next class, so revise the output size formula.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:08.500
<v Instructor>Next week's Deep Learning class has been moved from Thursday to Friday at the same time slot because of the faculty meeting.

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:15.900
<v Instructor>The midterm exam will cover everything up to and including this lecture on convolutional networks.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:23.400
<v Instructor>The exam is scheduled for October 5, and it will be held in the main hall.

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:30.800
<v Student>Will the exam include the pooling discussion?

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:37.500
<v Instructor>Yes, everything we discussed today is examinable, including the trade-off between pooling and strided convolutions.

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:44.900
<v Instructor>The reason we care about parameter sharing is that it reduces the number of weights dramatically compared to a dense layer.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.300
<v Instructor>Fully connected layers do not scale to image sized inputs, because the number of parameters explodes.

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:59.700
<v Instructor>Early layers learn edges and textures, while deeper layers learn shapes and eventually whole objects.

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:07.100
<v Instructor>You should read chapter seven of the textbook before the next session, and prepare two questions per group.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:14.600
<v Instructor>Office hours this week are extended by thirty minutes so that you can ask about the assignment.

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:21.900
<v Instructor>The final decision about the project dataset will be announced on the course page by Friday.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:29.400
<v Instructor>To summarize, convolution extracts local features, pooling or striding reduces resolution, and stacking builds a hierarchy.

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:36.800
<v Instructor>Are there any questions before we finish?

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:44.200
<v Student>Can you explain the difference between valid and same padding?

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:51.600
<v Instructor>Valid padding means no padding at all, so the output shrinks. Same padding keeps the output the same size as the input.

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:58.900
